course: dictionary
lesson: 8575
title: Many meanings: scheme
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and «scheme» is one of them. The words around it are its context, and they show you which meaning fits. «The school reading scheme helps every child learn to read.» Here a scheme is an official plan or system set up to help people do something.
explain: The same word can mean a secret or sneaky plan. «The thieves made a scheme to steal the jewels.» The clue is steal, so scheme means a dishonest plan, not a kind one. Try swapping in the word plan and check: it still fits, but the clue tells you it is a bad plan.
explain: Scheme can also be a verb, meaning to make secret plans, often to trick people. Look at the sentence to find out which meaning fits. If you are not sure, read the sentence again with your meaning in place of the hard word. If it still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
fact: Scheme can be a noun (a scheme, schemes) or a verb (to scheme, scheming).
fact: «Scheme» means an official plan or system, such as «a recycling scheme» or «a savings scheme».
fact: «Scheme» can also mean a clever, often secret or dishonest plan, such as «a scheme to steal the jewels».
fact: A «colour scheme» is a set of colours chosen to go together.
fact: In Scotland, a scheme can mean a housing estate. A «get-rich-quick scheme» is often a scam, so be careful with offers that promise money fast.
exercise: «The school reading scheme helps every child learn to read.» What does scheme mean here? | A set of colours that go together | A housing estate in Scotland | *An official plan or system for doing something | A secret plan to trick people
exercise: «The villain began to scheme in his dark tower.» What does scheme mean here? | *To make secret plans, often to trick people | To read a book quietly | To give away free food | To climb a tall hill
exercise: What does «colour scheme» mean? | A secret plan to steal paint | *A set of colours chosen to go together | A place where paint is sold | A housing estate
test: «Ben's class joined a cycle-to-school scheme.» What does scheme mean here? | A type of bicycle | A secret meeting | A trick to get money fast | *An official plan to help people do something
test: Why should you be careful with a «get-rich-quick scheme»? | It needs a new bicycle | It is the same as a school reading scheme | It always makes you rich, so it is safe | *It may be a scam that tricks people
exam: «In the grand scheme of things, one lost pencil does not matter.» What does scheme mean in this phrase? | A set of matching colours | *The overall way everything fits together | A housing estate | A secret plan to steal
